{{/* website-onboarding prompt templates. Rendered by text/template from
     pkg/internal/mcp/prompts.go. Template names carry a website_ prefix so they
     do not collide with the setup_* templates loaded into the same namespace
     by ParseFS. Data is the sitePromptData struct. */}}

{{define "website_overview"}}You are guiding a user through the website creation wizard.

Workflow overview (9 steps):
  1. auth_check     : verifies the user is authenticated
  2. content_source : choose CID or upload
  3. target_type    : IPFS or IPNS
  4. domain         : domain source + domain (custom or platform subdomain)
  5. dns_mode       : managed or self-managed DNS
  6. create         : create the website (confirm first)
  7. dns_setup      : read DNS requirements resource
  8. validate       : run live validation
  9. complete       : summarize results

Domain source (step 4):
  - DEFAULT — no domain preference: pass {"source": "platform_subdomain", "generate": true}
    to websites_wizard_step. The platform auto-generates a label, picks an available root,
    and builds the FQDN. DNS is managed by the platform. This is the correct choice for a
    generic website creation or publish request.
  - User explicitly supplied or requested a specific label (e.g. "call it acme"):
    pass {"source": "platform_subdomain", "label": "<label>"}. The wizard checks availability
    and builds the exact FQDN (label.root). Only use this when the user named a label.
  - User owns a custom domain: pass {"domain": "<user's domain>"}.
    Only use when the user explicitly provided a domain they own.
  - Do not infer a desire for custom naming from a generic request to create or publish
    a website.

Rules:
  - Always pass the session_id from websites_wizard_start to websites_wizard_step.
  - The next_step_schema in each response tells you the exact input shape.
  - Every step returns an in-band StepResponse carrying next_step_schema. To advance, call the step tool again passing JSON `input` matching that schema. No native form is emitted, so do not wait for one.
  - If a step returns an error, the session stays in the same state: you can retry.
  - Read pinner:// resources for live data (DNS requirements, platform domains, validation status).
{{- if .Domain}}

Pre-filled: domain={{.Domain}}
{{- end}}
{{end}}

{{define "website_auth_status"}}Read the pinner://account/status resource above. If "authenticated" is false, instruct the user to run `pinner auth` or provide an auth token via --auth-token. Do not proceed with the wizard until authentication is confirmed.
{{end}}

{{define "website_start"}}Call the `websites_wizard_start` tool (no arguments). It returns a session_id and the first step (auth_check). Store the session_id: you will pass it to every subsequent `websites_wizard_step` call.
{{end}}

{{define "website_step_auth_check"}}Step: auth_check
Call `websites_wizard_step` with the session_id and an empty input object {}. This step auto-validates the configured auth token. If it returns an error, stop and tell the user authentication is required.
{{end}}

{{define "website_step_content_source_upload"}}Step: content_source
Content source is "upload". The wizard requires an existing CID. Instruct the user to upload first: if using the MCP tools, call upload_file with wrap=true and do NOT set an explicit name — the tool auto-names wrapped HTML to index.html so the site resolves at its root. An explicit name like "starter-site" is honored as-is and the page will only be reachable at /starter-site, not /. If using the CLI, run `pinner upload --wrap <file>`. Then restart the wizard with content_source set to "cid". Call `websites_wizard_step` with input: {"choice": "upload"} (this will return an error with instructions).
{{end}}

{{define "website_step_content_source_cid"}}Step: content_source
Content source is "cid". Ask the user for their IPFS CID, then call `websites_wizard_step` with input: {"choice": "cid", "cid": "<CID>"}
{{end}}

{{define "website_step_content_source_ask"}}Step: content_source
Ask the user whether they have an IPFS CID ready ("cid") or need to upload content first ("upload"). If they choose "upload", they must upload first and restart. When uploading a single HTML file for a website, use wrap=true and do NOT set an explicit name — the tool auto-names wrapped HTML to index.html so the site resolves at its root. An explicit name like "starter-site" is honored as-is and the page will only be reachable at /starter-site, not /. The CID must be a directory containing index.html at its root. Call `websites_wizard_step` with input: {"choice": "<cid|upload>", "cid": "<CID if cid>"}
{{end}}

{{define "website_step_target_type_filled"}}Step: target_type
Call `websites_wizard_step` with input: {"type": "{{.TargetType}}"}
Use "ipfs" for immutable content-addressed data or "ipns" for mutable names.
{{end}}

{{define "website_step_target_type_ask"}}Step: target_type
Ask the user whether they want IPFS (immutable, content-addressed) or IPNS (mutable name). Call `websites_wizard_step` with input: {"type": "<ipfs|ipns>"}
{{end}}

{{define "website_step_domain_filled"}}Step: domain
Call `websites_wizard_step` with input: {"domain": "{{.Domain}}"}
{{end}}

{{define "website_step_domain_ask"}}Step: domain
Determine how the user wants to address the website:
  - DEFAULT — no domain preference (e.g. "create me a website", "publish this"): call
    `websites_wizard_step` with input: {"source": "platform_subdomain", "generate": true}.
    The platform auto-generates a subdomain label, picks an available root, builds the FQDN,
    creates the website, and claims the subdomain with platform-managed DNS. Do not invent
    a label or call websites_platform_domain_availability.
  - User explicitly supplied or requested a specific label (e.g. "call it acme"): list the
    enabled platform roots via pinner://websites/platform-domains (or websites_platform_domains_list),
    then confirm the label is claimable with `websites_platform_domain_availability <label>`,
    then call `websites_wizard_step` with input: {"source": "platform_subdomain", "label": "<label>"}.
    Only use this path when the user explicitly named a label.
  - Own domain: ask for their domain name (e.g. example.com) and pass
    {"domain": "<domain>"} (custom is inferred when a domain is given).
{{end}}

{{define "website_step_dns_mode_filled"}}Step: dns_mode
Call `websites_wizard_step` with input: {"mode": "{{.DNSMode}}"}
Use "managed" if Pinner should handle DNS, or "self_managed" if the user will configure DNS records themselves.
{{end}}

{{define "website_step_dns_mode_ask"}}Step: dns_mode
Ask the user whether they want managed DNS (Pinner handles it) or self-managed DNS (they configure records). Call `websites_wizard_step` with input: {"mode": "<managed|self_managed>"}
{{end}}

{{define "website_step_create"}}Step: create
Confirm with the user before proceeding. Call `websites_wizard_step` with input: {"confirm": true} to create the website. If the user is not ready, call with {"confirm": false} to see the error and retry.
{{end}}

{{define "website_step_dns_setup_filled"}}Read the pinner://websites/{domain}/dns-requirements resource above. Present the DNS records to the user. If DNS is managed, instruct them to update their registrar's nameservers. If self-managed, instruct them to add the TXT, dnslink, and optional CNAME records. Call `websites_wizard_step` with input: {} to proceed.
{{end}}

{{define "website_step_dns_setup_ask"}}Step: dns_setup
Read the pinner://websites/{domain}/dns-requirements resource (substitute the domain from the previous step) to get the exact DNS records. Present them to the user. Call `websites_wizard_step` with input: {} to proceed.
{{end}}

{{define "website_step_validate"}}Read the pinner://websites/{id}/validation-status resource (use the website ID from the create step's response). If validation fails, instruct the user to wait for DNS propagation (may take minutes to hours) and retry. Call `websites_wizard_step` with input: {"retry": true} to re-run validation. Call with input: {} to accept the current status and finish.
{{end}}

{{define "website_step_complete"}}When the wizard returns "complete": true, the website onboarding is done. Summarize the result for the user: domain, CID, target type, DNS mode, and validation status. If validation failed, provide the reason and suggest next steps. DNS validation confirms reachability, not content correctness — if the root URL serves a directory listing or 404 instead of the expected page, the CID's directory layout is likely wrong (e.g. missing index.html at the root).
{{end}}

{{/* website-update prompt templates. Rendered when updating an existing website's
     content. These drive the agent through websites_get / websites_update /
     websites_validate directly (no wizard session) while preserving the current
     target type and handling DNS per hosting mode. */}}

{{define "website_update_overview"}}You are updating an existing website's content.

Workflow overview (5 steps):
  1. resolve_state : Get the website config; capture target_type and dns_hosting_enabled
  2. ensure_pinned : Confirm the new CID is pinned on the gateway
  3. update        : Run websites_update preserving the current target type
  4. dns_check     : Branch on managed vs self-managed DNS
  5. verify        : Re-check the website and validation status

Rules:
  - Never guess target_type. If not supplied, run websites_get first and preserve the
    site's current type. Flipping IPFS<->IPNS without request breaks DNS resolution.
  - Do not call websites_update until the new CID is confirmed pinned.
  - For managed DNS, an invalid validation right after the update is usually DNS
    reconciliation lag, not a failure: wait briefly and re-check.{{if .WebsiteArg}}

Target website: {{.WebsiteArg}}{{end}}{{if .CID}}

New CID: {{.CID}}{{end}}
{{end}}

{{define "website_update_step_resolve"}}Step: resolve_state
{{- if .CurrentType}}
Current target type is {{.CurrentType}} (supplied). Preserve it on update unless you
have an explicit instruction to change IPFS<->IPNS.
{{- else}}
Call the `websites_get` tool with website="{{.WebsiteArg}}" (or read
pinner://websites/{{.WebsiteArg}}/dns-requirements) and extract:
  - target_type (ipfs or ipns) — this must be preserved
  - dns_hosting_enabled (true = managed DNS, false = self-managed)
If the site is not found, stop and report that the website does not exist (use the
create flow instead).
{{- end}}
{{end}}

{{define "website_update_step_pin"}}Step: ensure_pinned
Before updating, verify CID {{.CID}} is pinned on the gateway.
Check `pins_status` for the CID; if it is not pinned, call `pins_add` with
cids=["{{.CID}}"] and wait=true. Do not proceed to the update step until pinning
succeeds. An unpinned CID makes the update fail with CID_NOT_PINNED.
{{end}}

{{define "website_update_step_update"}}Step: update
Call the `websites_update` tool:
  website: "{{.WebsiteArg}}"
  cid:     "{{.CID}}"
{{- if .CurrentType}}
  target-type: "{{.CurrentType}}"
{{- end}}
Do not change dns-hosting unless explicitly requested; if target-type was not
resolved in the resolve_state step, omit it so the site's current type is
preserved automatically.
{{end}}

{{define "website_update_step_dns"}}Step: dns_check
If managed DNS (dns_hosting_enabled is true): do not touch DNS records. Pinner
reconciles the _dnslink record asynchronously, so validation may report the old
CID for a short time. Proceed to verify and wait before re-checking.
If self-managed DNS: read pinner://websites/{{.WebsiteArg}}/dns-requirements for
the expected _dnslink TXT value, and instruct the user to publish it for the new
CID. Validation can only pass after that record is live.
{{end}}

{{define "website_update_step_validate"}}Call the `websites_validate` tool (or read the pinner://websites/{id}/validation-status resource above) to confirm DNS resolves for the new CID.
If valid, proceed to complete.
If invalid because the _dnslink still references the old CID and DNS is managed,
wait ~30-60 seconds, then re-check once (reconciliation lag) before treating it as a failure.
If the error mentions an unpinned CID (CID_NOT_PINNED), go back to the ensure_pinned step.
{{end}}

{{define "website_update_step_complete"}}Update workflow finished.
Summarize the new CID ({{.CID}}){{if .CurrentType}}, target type preserved ({{.CurrentType}}){{end}}, and the current
validation status. If DNS is still reconciling, advise checking again in a few minutes.
{{end}}
